      "Empirical corporate-finance research consistently demonstrates that disciplined portfolio pruning—via strategic trade sales, tax-free shareholder spin-offs, or partial equity carve-outs—creates substantially more shareholder value than serial acquisitions. Yet executive teams routinely suffer from 'divestment inertia and the sunk-cost endowment fallacy'. Because selling a long-held division feels psychologically like an admission of past strategic error, shrinks headline consolidated revenue, and reduces the number of senior executive direct reports, boards frequently retain capital-hungry underperformers years after their strategic synergy with the core group has evaporated.",
