"Furthermore, in negotiations involving public-private partnerships, sovereign infrastructure concessions, or regulated utilities, private commercial confidentiality collides directly with 'democratic accountability and anti-corruption transparency mandates'. Increasingly, public-procurement statutes, freedom-of-information laws, and multilateral development-bank guidelines mandate the post-award publication of core concession contracts, fiscal guarantees, and beneficial ownership—meaning negotiators who rely on sweeping confidentiality clauses to shield uncompetitive pricing or off-balance-sheet public liabilities risk subsequent judicial invalidation of the entire agreement.",
"Comparative corporate-governance practice demonstrates that premier institutions resolve the confidentiality-versus-transparency dilemma through 'temporal and structural compartmentalisation'. By distinguishing rigorously between the 'exploratory negotiation phase' (where disciplined confidentiality protects candid creative problem-solving and market integrity) and the 'ratification and execution phase' (where clear, plain-language disclosure of material financial terms, risk allocations, and governance structures satisfies shareholders, regulators, and public stakeholders), boards honour both commercial prudence and institutional accountability.",
