"titre": "Decision-Making Complexity in Matrix Organisations and Decentralised Structures",
"titre2": "Exercise 52",
"texte": [
"Organisational Design & Governance Diagnostic — Prepared for the Group Executive Board of Aurelia-Siemens Industrial Systems SE — Five years after transitioning from a traditional regional-subsidiary hierarchy to a global multi-dimensional 'matrix organisation'—crossing four global product divisions, three geographic regions, and six shared functional platforms—our internal operational velocity review has exposed acute 'decision-making paralysis and coordination fatigue'. Bringing a customised industrial automation solution to market now requires an average of nineteen cross-functional alignment meetings and eleven dual-line sign-offs, while senior product and country executives spend over 55 % of their working week in internal consensus committees rather than with customers.",
"In classic organisational theory pioneered by Jay Galbraith and Christopher Bartlett, matrix structures were engineered to solve a genuine strategic dilemma: how a global enterprise can simultaneously capture 'worldwide product-line R&D economies of scale' and 'deep local market responsiveness'. Yet when dual-reporting lines ('solid-line and dotted-line accountability') are implemented without unambiguous decision-rights allocation, horizontal coordination rapidly degenerates into structural ambiguity.",
"The first pathology of poorly governed matrix structures is 'ambiguous authority and shadow veto power'. Whenever a regional sales director and a global product-line vice-president hold overlapping authority over pricing, engineering customisation, or capital allocation without a clear tie-breaking rule, every disagreement escalates upward to the executive board—or worse, stalls indefinitely in polite committee limbo because neither leader possesses unilateral authority to decide while both hold informal power to block.",
"Simultaneously, decentralised and matrix organisations suffer from 'cognitive overload, meeting proliferation, and diffused accountability'. Because matrix managers fear being blindsided by stakeholders across other axes, they invite dozens of peripheral observers to every operational review—turning lean working sessions into bloated consensus assemblies where everyone is consulted, nobody owns the outcome, and execution speed collapses.",
"Empirical studies of high-performing global enterprises—including leading Nordic and Swiss industrial-technology groups—demonstrate that matrix complexity is mastered not by oscillating endlessly between centralization and decentralization, but through 'explicit decision-rights architecture, single-point P&L accountability, and behavioural collaboration norms'. By assigning unambiguous primary ownership ('Decider' role) for every recurring strategic and operational decision category, keeping matrix reporting strictly two-dimensional rather than three- or four-dimensional, and cultivating shared cross-axis incentives, premier firms capture global scale without bureaucratic drag.",
"The subtle irony of matrix organisations is that structures created to promote horizontal collaboration and agile cross-unit synergy frequently end up generating far more internal friction, turf politics, and delay than the rigid vertical silos they replaced.",
"To restore crisp decision velocity and unambiguous accountability across Aurelia-Siemens Industrial Systems without forfeiting global scale or local agility, this diagnostic recommends four structural governance reforms: first, simplifying our three-dimensional matrix into a clean 'Primary-Axis P&L Structure' where global business units own product strategy and R&D while regional hubs own customer execution within pre-agreed pricing corridors; second, codifying an enterprise-wide 'Decision-Rights Charter' ('RAPID' framework) naming a single Decider for every core operational workflow; third, capping standing cross-functional coordination committees by 50 %; and fourth, aligning executive bonuses 70 % to group-and-axis joint outcomes.",
"Ultimately, an organisational chart is never a substitute for leadership clarity: matrix and decentralised structures succeed only when people know unmistakably who decides, who contributes, and who answers for results."
