"Simultaneously, directors face heightened personal exposure during periods of 'acute financial distress and insolvency-zone governance'. Across European jurisdictions—including Germany's strict statutory 'Insolvenzantragspflicht' (requiring filing within twenty-one days of illiquidity) and the UK's wrongful-trading regime—as well as common-law creditor-duty rulings, directors who allow a company to continue incurring liabilities or distributing dividends when insolvency is probable risk personal civil restitution orders, tax-arrears liability, and criminal prosecution.",
"Furthermore, reliance on 'Directors and Officers ('D&O') liability insurance' and corporate indemnification clauses provides far narrower protection than many board members assume. Standard D&O policies routinely exclude fraud, deliberate statutory breaches, regulatory fines, pollution liabilities, and 'insured-versus-insured' claims—and in severe corporate insolvencies, Side-A non-indemnifiable coverage limits can be rapidly exhausted by multi-party defence costs before individual non-executive directors reach trial.",
