      "The primary analytical obstacle in valuing intangible assets during fundraising lies in 'accounting invisibility and non-linear payoff optionality'. Under standard accounting frameworks ('IAS 38' and 'US GAAP'), internally developed algorithms, training datasets, customer-acquisition cohorts, and brand equity are expensed immediately rather than capitalised—producing negative reported EBITDA precisely when an emerging enterprise is compounding its most valuable intellectual capital. Simultaneously, intangible assets exhibit 'winner-take-most convexity': if an algorithmic architecture achieves technical dominance or regulatory approval, its marginal reproduction cost approaches zero; if superseded by a rival standard, its liquidation recovery value collapses near zero.",
