      "Pan-African Capital Markets & Regional Integration Briefing — Prepared for the AfCFTA Investment & Financial Integration Council — With the operationalisation of the African Continental Free Trade Area ('AfCFTA') and its landmark Investment Protocol uniting fifty-four countries into a 1.4-billion-person market, Africa possesses the demographic and industrial scale required for transformative economic integration. Yet while merchandise tariff schedules are progressively liberalising, intra-African cross-border investment still accounts for less than 15 % of total continental foreign direct investment—held back by persistent 'institutional, regulatory, and payment-infrastructure barriers to the free movement of capital' across Africa's forty-two distinct national currencies and fragmented securities exchanges.",
      "The first structural barrier fragmenting intra-African capital mobility lies in 'asymmetric capital-account controls, repatriation friction, and currency-convertibility bottlenecks'. Across several African jurisdictions outside established monetary unions such as CEMAC and WAEMU, cross-border equity injections, intra-group shareholder loans, and dividend repatriations remain subject to discretionary central-bank approvals, multi-month foreign-exchange allocation queues, and punitive withholding taxes—deterring regional institutional pension funds and pan-African corporate groups from deploying patient growth capital across neighbouring borders.",
