      "Furthermore, cross-border corporate treasury and trade settlements suffer from 'correspondent-banking routing inefficiencies'. Historically, settling a commercial invoice or capital transfer between a manufacturer in Cameroon or Côte d'Ivoire and a buyer in Kenya or Zambia required routing funds through third-party correspondent banks in London, New York, or Paris—incurring double foreign-exchange conversion spreads, multi-day settlement delays, and an estimated $5 billion in annual continental transaction leakage.",
