"Industrial Transformation & Value-Chain Strategy Briefing — Prepared for the Pan-African Mineral, Agro-Industrial & Energy Beneficiation Council — Accounting for over 30 % of global critical-mineral reserves—including cobalt, manganese, platinum-group metals, bauxite, lithium, and copper—alongside more than 60 % of the world's uncultivated arable land and cocoa, coffee, and cashew production, Africa remains structurally trapped at the lowest-margin upstream tier of global value chains. Exporting unprocessed bauxite, raw cocoa beans, or unrefined lithium concentrates captures less than 10 % to 15 % of final downstream retail or battery-pack value, while exposing national economies to violent commodity price swings. Transforming raw-material abundance into durable industrial prosperity requires mastering 'local processing ('beneficiation') and downstream value capture'.",
"Understanding why raw-material beneficiation has historically stalled requires moving beyond simplistic political slogans to address 'hard industrial-economics prerequisites'. Smelting bauxite into aluminium, refining battery-grade lithium hydroxide, or manufacturing finished chocolate and specialty textiles requires reliable, competitively priced baseload electricity, integrated heavy-rail and port logistics, skilled chemical and metallurgical engineers, and proximate regional off-take scale—conditions frequently absent when export bans are imposed abruptly without parallel infrastructure investment.",
