      "Public Enterprise Reform & Corporate Governance Note — Prepared for the Pan-African State-Owned Enterprise ('SOE') Oversight & Fiscal Governance Forum — Across African economies, state-owned enterprises occupy commanding heights in electric power utilities, national airlines, port authorities, railway corridors, telecommunications backbones, and development banks—controlling assets exceeding 30 % of continental GDP. Yet recurrent fiscal audits reveal that underperforming commercial SOEs absorb up to 2 % to 4 % of national GDP annually in direct treasury bailouts, uncalled sovereign loan guarantees, and unpaid tax arrears while delivering unreliable grid power and high logistics costs that penalise private-sector competitiveness. Reconciling public-service mandates with rigorous 'economic performance and commercial governance' is therefore one of the continent's most urgent institutional priorities.",
      "In institutional economics and OECD Guidelines on Corporate Governance of State-Owned Enterprises, chronic SOE underperformance stems primarily from 'the dual-principal conflict and soft budget constraints'. When a line ministry simultaneously acts as sector regulator, political patron, social-welfare distributor, and controlling shareholder, commercial efficiency is systematically subordinated to short-term political imperatives—such as maintaining below-cost retail utility tariffs, absorbing patronage payroll expansion, and awarding non-competitive procurement contracts.",
      "Simultaneously, SOE operational discipline erodes under 'ambiguous mandate accounting and uncompensated Public Service Obligations ('PSOs')'. When a national power utility or railway operator is compelled by ministerial decree to supply subsidised electricity or operate loss-making rural routes without transparent parliamentary budget reimbursement, operating losses accumulate opaquely—masking genuine managerial inefficiency behind legitimate social-mandate deficits and preventing objective executive accountability.",
      "Furthermore, weak board autonomy and politicised executive appointments undermine 'long-term capital-allocation discipline and balance-sheet solvency'. Boards populated by civil-servant representatives lacking independent commercial, audit, or engineering expertise—and chief executives replaced with every cabinet reshuffle—cannot execute multi-year turnaround plans, enforce preventive maintenance, or access international capital markets without explicit sovereign guarantees.",
      "Empirical reform successes across leading emerging and African public enterprises—such as Ethiopian Airlines' commercial governance autonomy, Morocco's OCP Group corporate transformation, and central-ownership holding models—demonstrate that high-performing SOEs combine 'centralised professional ownership, ring-fenced commercial boards, explicit PSO budget compensation, and partial minority private-capital discipline'. Separating state ownership into an independent professional holding entity insulated from sectoral ministries allows SOEs to compete commercially while delivering strategic national infrastructure.",
